-R9 addresses the live R8 data-plane stall reproduced under forced leg1-first + 3s slow-leg0 upload testing. R8 correctly stopped blaming healthy Hy2 for a leg0-owned cumulative-ACK frontier, but the sender could still pin the frontier in `inTransit` on a delayed/backpressured leg0 writer. Because the old retry path skipped every `inTransit` record, the same seq could not be replayed on leg1; the 1024-frame inflight window then filled and application forward progress stalled.
+R10 addresses the remaining R9 upload-throughput scheduling bottleneck observed in the live 500 MiB upload benchmark. R9 completed the full upload correctly (`HTTP=200`, 524288000 bytes) and no longer entered `tx_ack_stall` / fallback, but the sender often kept `tx_outstanding=1024` while `frontier_rescues` advanced at roughly the 250ms retransmit-ticker cadence.
+
+R10 keeps rescue semantics deliberately narrow: only the proven cumulative blocker `outstanding[ackedNext]` may receive a concurrent alternate-leg DATA attempt. It does not introduce a fixed rescue burst because SMP3 v4 has cumulative ACK but no SACK.
+
+Implementation changes:
+- cumulative ACK progress coalesces an O(1) `frontierRescueCh` wake;
+- `scheduleFrontierRescue()` is separated from the full outstanding retry scan;
+- timer / leg-repair events retain ordinary retry recovery plus frontier safety-net checking;
+- non-overdue ACK wakeups return before taking a transport snapshot/allocation;
+- rescue cooldown is committed only after the priority queue accepts the attempt; failed/abandoned rescue attempts roll the current cooldown back;
+- a failed concrete leg generation invalidates outstanding `lastSentLeg` ownership for that ID, so same-ID replacements replay old DATA correctly;
+- dead-leg retry candidates are sorted by sequence so cumulative-ACK frontier DATA is replayed before future frames;
+- `retransmit_timeout`, rescue queue size, wire version, config schema, and adaptive thresholds remain unchanged.
 
